#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76210bf0-b3ae-5675-aa60-65ed8fba1577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76210bf0-b3ae-5675-aa60-65ed8fba1577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304389} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f42fa8-490c-526d-b6af-3979ac2774c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0898607-40ba-5160-b291-bbfc5674ac89" :question/math-academy-id "q-342242" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae63895f-26a5-550a-9e82-7414623e5158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063335} {:answer/id #uuid "df6a43b6-1b71-5020-aa36-4c0f562445ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1^{-})f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063336} {:answer/id #uuid "19140e5f-24a2-5763-ba7e-b7d994f1db7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1)f(x) \\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063337} {:answer/id #uuid "2403be5a-750d-57cf-99a8-51653ca8fe77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063338} {:answer/id #uuid "32cdefd9-4378-561b-a0cd-f3b8e222c2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df6a43b6-1b71-5020-aa36-4c0f562445ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1^{-})f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063336} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a26449-9faf-5a21-a528-5c30e4b24664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca98ec7d-0763-558c-b634-f1da93c05079" :question/math-academy-id "q-51054" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676972b0-9c38-555e-aa6c-98b2576391e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304392} {:answer/id #uuid "71a6bb71-4604-51f2-8c37-2d5b05a0a08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304393} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9e4522-c31d-5a7f-bac7-b6645a714993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304394} {:answer/id #uuid "8450d8cf-2a55-5c0b-b6ca-41a99c6809a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304395} {:answer/id #uuid "8e63c9f9-177f-5b35-8632-17b0012d74b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8450d8cf-2a55-5c0b-b6ca-41a99c6809a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304395} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6edc00b-3d38-589c-898c-269b98486106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22f74da2-9345-55bb-831c-5f5f24cacef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71068" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8,$ then what is the value of $\\sqrt{f(-1)+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d55ffa54-16ff-5309-9227-f9eddcec0dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304399} {:answer/id #uuid "92725a5b-0cb3-5c0a-b256-4d7c8498acb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304400} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ea99a2-5320-550c-b408-59fb4d23af63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304401} {:answer/id #uuid "d58972ba-c40c-53b6-bc5e-7b0121468430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304402} {:answer/id #uuid "5af31e22-830a-53c3-8f12-4cacbb72a828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92725a5b-0cb3-5c0a-b256-4d7c8498acb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304400} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac9bc9e-08a9-59ce-9726-fe81c86b198c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eeea373f-95bb-5842-a9f9-990d433fa820" :question/math-academy-id "q-71071" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=2,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a211603e-ede9-52c2-a8e1-d000c64df542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304406} {:answer/id #uuid "8401d42d-29e4-51cb-be08-d69ea97c9132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304407} {:answer/id #uuid "02592502-597f-51a4-ac46-8f134902e392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304408} {:answer/id #uuid "dc732819-aa43-5652-923d-8022d0e979a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304409} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ae7c5-8d21-5195-8f84-1afce5f07966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a211603e-ede9-52c2-a8e1-d000c64df542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304406} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bde795-7c9d-5fcf-adaa-aa6a537785b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5fc9af3-149c-5207-9936-917f743c751e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71080" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-2x+3, & x<2 \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist."}]}
